    {
      "id": "ACP-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The cup decomposition extends a polynomial annihilator to every translation class",
      "anchor": "inputs-additive-action",
      "statement": "On the additive group of rectangular integral matrices, if a polynomial Fⱼ(v*) kills the cup words as cochains, applying it to the cup decomposition gives Fⱼ(v*)c=δ(Fⱼ(v*)e).",
      "hypotheses": "Here v* is pullback by entrywise multiplication by v with the operator in the degree on which it acts. The affine application has a totally complex field and 1≤j≤m<r₂. The concrete Fⱼ(1)≠0 and its vanishing on words are separate arithmetic and eigenvalue inputs.",
      "definitions": [
        "Pullback acts on c using q+2 vertices and on e using q+1 vertices.",
        "The unit and polynomial hypotheses enter the application after generation has been established in all positive degrees."
      ],
